---
title: 'Take-Home Assignment vs Live Coding vs Pair Programming: Choosing a Technical Assessment'
publishedAt: 2026-11-11
type: companies
---
**Quick answer:** Choose the technical assessment that looks most like the real job and respects the candidate's time. **Take-home assignments** (2–4 hours) show realistic code quality but take candidates' evenings, so keep them short or pay for them. **Live coding** (45–60 minutes) is quick and shows problem solving under time pressure, but can unfairly penalise nervous candidates. **Pair programming** (60–90 minutes) on a realistic task shows how someone actually works with a teammate and is often the best signal for product engineering roles. Whatever you choose, use a written rubric, tell candidates what to expect, and allow AI tools if your team uses them at work.

The technical assessment is usually the most expensive step in tech hiring, for you and for the candidate. Get it wrong and you'll either lose strong people who won't spend a weekend on your assignment, or hire people who are good at puzzles but struggle with real work.

