## Take-home assignments

A take-home is a small project the candidate builds on their own time, then submits for review.

**Pros:**

- Candidates work in their own environment, with fewer nerves.
- You see realistic code: structure, tests, naming, README.
- Good for people who think best without an audience.

**Cons:**

- Senior candidates and people with caregiving responsibilities often drop out.
- Scope creep: a "2-hour" task often takes 8.
- Harder to know who, or what, wrote the code.

**How to do it well:**

- **Cap the time** and say so: "Please spend no more than 3 hours. We'll judge it with that in mind."
- **Pay for longer assignments.** It signals respect and widens your pool.
- **Use a realistic, small task**, like "Add pagination and filtering to this API", not "Build a clone of our product."
- **Give a starter repository**, so candidates don't spend an hour on setup.
- **Follow up with a 30-minute review call** where they walk you through their choices and you ask what they'd change. This also confirms they understand the code.

## Live coding interviews

The candidate solves one or two problems while sharing their screen or in a shared editor, talking through their approach.

**Pros:**

- Fast and easy to schedule.
- Shows how someone breaks down a problem and responds to hints.
- Hard to outsource.

**Cons:**

- Pressure affects performance, often more than skill does.
- Algorithm puzzles rarely reflect day-to-day work.
- Can favour people who've spent months practising puzzle sites.

**How to do it well:**

- **Use practical problems**: parsing a log file, building a small function with edge cases, fixing a failing test. Avoid trick questions.
- **Let candidates choose their language.**
- **Allow them to look things up.** Nobody memorises every API.
- **Give hints** and score how they use them.
- **Start with a warm-up** question to settle nerves.

## Pair programming sessions

The candidate and an engineer from your team work together on a realistic task in a real codebase (or a simplified copy).

**Pros:**

- The closest thing to working together.
- Shows communication, how they ask questions, how they take suggestions.
- Gives candidates a real look at your codebase and team.

**Cons:**

- Needs a well-prepared task and a trained interviewer.
- The interviewer's style affects the candidate's performance.

**How to do it well:**

- **Prepare a task with a clear goal** and room to go further, such as "Add rate limiting to this endpoint."
- **Explain the roles**: "You drive, I'll navigate and answer questions like a teammate would."
- **Score collaboration and approach**, not only whether they finish.

## What about AI coding tools?

Most engineering teams now use AI coding assistants every day. Decide your policy and tell candidates in advance:

- **Allowed (recommended for most roles):** you're testing how they work in practice. Ask them to explain any generated code and how they checked it.
- **Not allowed:** useful when you specifically want to test fundamentals, for example for very junior roles. Say so clearly.

Either way, the follow-up conversation matters more than ever. Ask "Why this approach?" and "What would break first in production?"

## Which assessment should you use?

- **Founding or senior product engineer:** pair programming, or a short paid take-home plus a review call.
- **Junior or graduate roles:** a short practical live coding exercise, with hints.
- **Infrastructure or SRE:** a debugging exercise on a broken system, done live or as pair programming.
- **Data roles:** a short take-home with a real dataset and a review call.
- **High-volume hiring:** a brief live exercise after an [AI screening interview](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/ai-screening-interviews-replace-30-phone-screens-with-one-page-reports).

## Use a rubric for every assessment

Decide what you're scoring before you see any submissions. A simple rubric for a backend take-home:

{% table %}
- Area
- What "strong" looks like
---
- Correctness
- Works for the main case and handles edge cases
---
- Code clarity
- Easy to read, sensible names and structure
---
- Testing
- Meaningful tests for important behaviour
---
- Design choices
- Reasonable trade-offs, explained in the README or call
---
- Communication
- Can explain what they did and what they'd change
{% /table %}

See our guide to [structured interviews and scorecards](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/structured-interviews-for-tech-hiring-how-to-build-scorecards-and-rubrics) for how to score and debrief.

## Tell candidates what to expect

Put the assessment format in your job post and invite email:

- What type of exercise it is and how long it takes.
- Which languages and tools they can use, including AI.
- What you're looking for.
- Whether it's paid.

On GetHired, you can add an assessment stage like "Take-home" or "Pair programming" to your [pipeline](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/a-lightweight-ats-for-startups-pipeline-stages-boards-notes-and-email-templates) and send the details with an email template when you move candidates into it.

## Frequently asked questions

### How long should a take-home assignment be?

Two to four hours at most. If it needs longer, pay candidates for their time.

### Are LeetCode-style interviews still useful?

They test algorithmic problem solving, which matters for some roles. For most product engineering jobs, practical exercises predict performance better.

### Should I pay candidates for take-home assignments?

It's increasingly common for longer assignments, and it helps you keep senior candidates and people with less free time in your process.

### How do I stop candidates cheating on take-homes?

Follow every take-home with a short review call where they explain and extend their code. Someone who didn't write or understand it will struggle.

### Can I skip technical assessments for senior hires?

Some teams replace them with a deep technical conversation about past work. It saves time but gives less direct evidence, so use a strong rubric.

